$\begin{align}
&\phantom{=}\dfrac{2-\sqrt{4x-12}}{x-4}
\\\\
&=\dfrac{2-\sqrt{4x-12}}{x-4}\cdot\dfrac{2+\sqrt{4x-12}}{2+\sqrt{4x-12}}\qquad\gray{\text{Rationalize the numerator}}
\\\\
&=\dfrac{2^2-(4x-12)}{(x-4)(2+\sqrt{4x-12})}
\\\\
&=\dfrac{-4\cancel{(x-4)}}{\cancel{(x-4)}(2+\sqrt{4x-12})}\qquad\gray{\text{Cancel out common factors}}
\\\\
&=\dfrac{-4}{2+\sqrt{4x-12}} \text{, for }x\neq 4
\end{align}$

This means that the two expressions have the same value for all $x$-values (in their domains) except for $4$.

We can now use the following theorem:

If $f(x)=g(x)$ for all $x$-values in a given interval except for $x=c$, then $\displaystyle\lim_{x\to c}f(x)=\lim_{x\to c}g(x)$.
